    "created_at": "2016-07-07T15:19:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-07T15:19:36Z",
    "body": "No, but that isn't the point - there is a spec for this, and you are \"wilfully violating\" it.  You are not the e-mail spec maintainters.  i'd love to have a more custom e-mail address but efforts like this only make doing to even harder!  I can understand giving people the option for a more restrictive definition of an e-mail address at a per-website level, but it shouldn't be the default - that is a decision that should be left to website operators.  Currently, there is NO WAY to use this system to correctly verify e-mails.  Even if you use the custom regex option, it is literally impossible to write a regex to fully validate e-mails.  So people who do want to correctly follow the real e-mail spec are left to implementing custom validation (possibly in javascript, which means it won't work for all people, at least a custom regex has slightly wider usage by being natively supported), and so can't use this form type for what it was originally intended anyway.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
