    "created_at": "2016-07-13T16:03:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-13T16:03:42Z",
    "body": "The justification for being tighter than RFC 5322/RFC 5321 is that, in practice, almost all of your stuff is going to break in downstream pipes anyways. RFC 5321 enforces that the right-hand side of an email production is a fully-qualified domain name (when publicly exposed) or an encoded IP address literal (with the full semantics of domain name), and constrains the local-part to end-node-interpretation-only. RFC 5321 does have SHOULD qualifiers on case-insensitive, not using local-parts that require quoted forms, and using minimal quoting. People who try to zealously match RFC 5322 are unlikely to realize that they then have a canonicalization issue--\"foo\"@bar.com and foo@bar.com are the same address, as is foo (this (is a (horrible) joke))@BaR.(ha-ha, there is more)cOm\n\nIn practice, unless you're working on SMTP deep guts, a case-preserving system that accepts the dot-atom-text production in the local part and a valid domain name on the right is correct. If you want to support EAI, it would be NFC-normalized local-part omitting C0 and C1 controls and the specials less dot.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
